"....From Black Computers to White - NeXTSTEP for Intel Processors"

The Toronto Area NeXT Group presents a sneak preview of the Intel port of  
the World's foremost object-oriented operating system.

The Battle of the Operating Systems heated up substantially on February  
10, 1993 when NeXT Computer, Inc. announced that it was getting out of the  
black box (NeXT hardware) business and becoming solely a software company.  
Although there has been a mixed reaction in the NeXT community about this  
change of direction, most NeXT users were just as attracted to the NeXT  
world by its operating system "NeXTSTEP" as much as by the sleak, black  
hardware. 

NeXTSTEP has a "super-Mac-like" graphical user interface, a wealth of  
powerful networking, communication and system administration utilities,  
and a dynamite application development environment. It would truly be a  
tragedy to see such an elegant operating system go down with a hardware  
ship. Anyway, it has been said that computer hardware is becoming merely a  
commodity. The real power and innovation in the computer industry is now  
in the hands of the software houses, and in particular those which control  
the direction of operating system development. 

The race is now on to see who will set the standard for operating systems  
which incorporate the philosophy of "object-oriented" programming and  
design. NeXTSTEP is the most advanced object-oriented system software  
environment available on the market today. With seven years of development  
behind it and 50,000 users, it holds a substantial lead over other  
potential object-oriented operating systems being developed by the  
Taligent Consortium of IBM and Apple, Microsoft ("Cairo"), and others. Up  
until this year, NeXTSTEP was only available to the select few who owned  
NeXT's black boxes....now it is to be let loose on the Intel  
processor-based world with the release of "NeXTSTEP for Intel" on May 25,  
1993 at the NeXTWoRLD ExPO in San Francisco. 

I guess you will just have to fly to sunny California in May to see  
it....NOT!

The Toronto Area NeXT Group has got its hot little hands on an advance  
copy of NeXTSTEP for Intel and is holding a special meeting at 7:00 pm  
Tuesday April 8, 1993 at the Toronto Eye Institute to introduce NeXTSTEP  
for Intel to Toronto. The Toronto Eye Institute is located on the sixth  
floor of the Toronto (Western) Hospital at the corner of Bathurst and  
Dundas, in Toronto. We welcome all computer enthusiasts of any stripe to  
join us. Jump the gun and see for yourself the future of computing!


