In article <C5x1CE.44E@mccc.edu>, werden@mccc.edu (Andrew J Werden dp270-5204) writes...
>Help!
> 
>	I've committed to migrating/converting an application to a VAX 7000?
>( not sure- haven't started yet) from an IBM 4361 running CICS 1.7 and
>DOS/SSX. Am hoping someone can tell me:
> 
>	1- What dialect of COBOL does the VAX speak (under VMS)?

$ HELP COBOL/STANDARD says that the 1985 ANSI standard is the default.  

>	2- Is this dialect supported by a PC-based development set? 
>	   (especially MicroFocus Workbench!)

Can you tell MicroFocus that you want to compose only in standard COBOL
85 syntax?  If so, you might be able to get somewhere.  Certainly the
MicroFocus tools that are CICS related won't help you much, though.  The
TP monitor on VMS is ACMS, not CICS.

>	3- Does the VAX have good tools to handle ASCII/EBCDIC (or can they
>	   be purchased?)

Certain basic support for ASCII/EBCDIC was designed in and bundled since
"forever".  For example, the run time library service lib$tra_ebc_asc
does translation of ebcdic to ascii.  I think that COBOL itself also has
some support within the language, though I've forgotten the clause.

>	4- Does anyone in the NYC area rent 6250bpi 9-tracks for short-term
>	   use (i.e. weekend data conversion!)
>	5- Does anyone in the NYC area rent CPU/disk on their VAXes?
>	6- Are there any good or free books on VMS and VAX Cobol that I can 
>	   get hold of?

You need both the "VAX COBOL Reference Manual" and the "VAX COBOL User
Manual".   Typically the VAX languages document certain essential things
(like how to call run time library services) in the "User Manual"

